# Full studies: the Eucharist, the Mass, priesthood, confession. Loaded by tools/vapol.py (exec).
# John 6 in the Douay is numbered one higher than most Bibles from 6:52 (KJV 6:51) onward.

D('john',6,64,
 ctx="John 6 opens with the feeding of the five thousand at Passover time (6:4). The next day Jesus teaches in the synagogue at Capernaum: ‘I am the bread of life’ (6:35, 48). Then he goes further: ‘the bread that I will give, is my flesh, for the life of the world’ (6:52). The Jews dispute: ‘How can this man give us his flesh to eat?’ Jesus does not soften it: ‘Amen, amen I say unto you: Except you eat the flesh of the Son of man, and drink his blood, you shall not have life in you. He that eateth my flesh, and drinketh my blood, hath everlasting life… For my flesh is meat indeed: and my blood is drink indeed’ (6:54–56). Many disciples say: ‘This saying is hard, and who can hear it?’ (6:61). Jesus answers: ‘Doth this scandalize you? If then you shall see the Son of man ascend up where he was before? It is the spirit that quickeneth: the flesh profiteth nothing. The words that I have spoken to you, are spirit and life. But there are some of you that believe not’ (6:62–65). ‘After this many of his disciples went back; and walked no more with him’ (6:67). Peter: ‘Lord, to whom shall we go? thou hast the words of eternal life’ (6:69).",
 who="Verse 64 (63 in most Bibles) is the main text for a symbolic or ‘spiritual’ reading of John 6. Zwingli made it central in his debate with Luther at Marburg (1529): Jesus himself explains that eating his flesh would profit nothing; his words are ‘spirit’, meaning they are to be understood spiritually, as believing in him. Eating = believing (6:35: ‘he that cometh to me shall not hunger: and he that believeth in me shall never thirst’). The discourse is about faith, not a sacrament.",
 lang="Verse numbers: Douay 6:52 = KJV 6:51; 6:54–59 = 6:53–58; 6:64 = 6:63; 6:67 = 6:66; 6:69 = 6:68.\n\nFrom 6:55 (KJV 6:54) Jesus switches from the ordinary verb for eating (phagein/esthiō) to trōgō, ‘to chew, munch’, four times (KJV 6:54, 56, 57, 58). It is a vivid physical verb; elsewhere in John only at 13:18 (‘he that eateth (trōgōn) bread with me’).\n\n“Meat indeed… drink indeed” (alēthēs brōsis… alēthēs posis): ‘true food… true drink’.\n\n“The flesh” in 6:64 is hē sarx, without ‘my’. In John ‘flesh’ alone means human nature left to itself, natural understanding: ‘That which is born of the flesh, is flesh’ (3:6); ‘You judge according to the flesh’ (8:15).\n\nIn Hebrew and Aramaic idiom, ‘to eat someone’s flesh’ figuratively meant to slander or attack them: ‘whilst the wicked draw near against me, to eat my flesh’ (Psalm 26:2, 27:2 in most Bibles); Micah 3:3.",
 steps=[
  ["Jesus does not say ‘my flesh profiteth nothing’","He says ‘the flesh’. His own flesh, he has just said, is ‘given for the life of the world’ (6:52). If his flesh profits nothing, the Incarnation and the Cross profit nothing. No Christian believes that. ‘The flesh’ here is the natural, unaided way of understanding (as in 3:6; 8:15), the way the crowd was hearing him: as if he meant cannibalism, cutting off pieces of a corpse."],
  ["‘Spirit and life’ means his words are true and life-giving","‘The words that I have spoken to you, are spirit and life.’ Spirit in John is not the opposite of ‘real’ or ‘physical’; God is spirit (4:24) and supremely real. Jesus says his words must be received by the Spirit, by faith, not by fleshly reasoning. That is why he adds: ‘there are some of you that believe not’ (6:65). The problem is not that they took him too literally; it is that they did not believe."],
  ["Jesus intensifies, he does not soften","When the crowd objects ‘How can this man give us his flesh to eat?’ (6:53), Jesus repeats the point more strongly six times, uses the physical verb trōgō, and says his flesh is ‘true food’. If he meant only ‘believe in me’, he could have said so and kept his disciples. Elsewhere, when people misunderstood a metaphor, Jesus corrected them (Matthew 16:6–12, ‘the leaven of the Pharisees’; John 3:3–5; 11:11–14)."],
  ["He lets disciples leave rather than explain it away","‘After this many of his disciples went back; and walked no more with him’ (6:67): the only time in the Gospels that disciples leave Jesus over a doctrine. He turns to the Twelve: ‘Will you also go away?’ He accepts losing them rather than say ‘I only meant it symbolically’. Peter does not understand either, but trusts: ‘thou hast the words of eternal life’."],
  ["A metaphorical reading would mean ‘slander me’","In the biblical idiom, eating someone’s flesh and drinking his blood figuratively meant to assault or revile him (Psalm 26:2; Micah 3:3; also Revelation 17:6, 16). If Jesus were speaking metaphorically, he would be saying ‘unless you revile me, you shall not have life’. The figure does not work."],
  ["The discourse has two parts","Catholics agree that the first part (6:35–51) is about believing in Jesus, the bread from heaven. But from 6:52 the gift shifts to the future (‘the bread that I will give’), the content to ‘my flesh… for the life of the world’, and the act from coming and believing to eating and drinking. The setting is Passover (6:4), when the lamb was eaten. The Last Supper fulfils it: ‘This is my body… This is my blood’."],
  ["The earliest Christians read it literally","See the Fathers below: Ignatius (c. 107), Justin (c. 155), Irenaeus (c. 180), Cyril of Jerusalem (c. 350). The symbolic reading of the Eucharist as only a sign appears in a developed form in the 9th century (Ratramnus, disputed) and 11th century (Berengar, who recanted), and became widespread only with Zwingli in the 1520s. Luther himself rejected Zwingli’s reading and insisted ‘This is my body’ means what it says (Marburg, 1529)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Ignatius of Antioch, Letter to the Smyrnaeans 7.1 (c. 107)","Of the Docetist heretics: ‘They abstain from the Eucharist and from prayer, because they confess not the Eucharist to be the flesh of our Saviour Jesus Christ, which suffered for our sins.’"],
  ["Justin Martyr, First Apology 66 (c. 155)","‘Not as common bread and common drink do we receive these… the food which is blessed by the prayer of his word… is the flesh and blood of that Jesus who was made flesh.’"],
  ["Irenaeus, Against Heresies V.2.2–3 (c. 180)","The bread which receives the word of God becomes the Eucharist, the body of Christ, and our bodies nourished by it are prepared for resurrection (paraphrased)."],
  ["Cyril of Jerusalem, Mystagogical Catecheses 4.6 (c. 350)","‘Do not regard them as bare bread and wine; for they are, according to the Lord’s declaration, the body and blood of Christ. Though sense suggests this to you, let faith establish you’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, Tractates on John 27.5 (c. 414)","On 6:64: they understood the flesh carnally, as if it were to be cut up like meat in the market; ‘the flesh profiteth nothing’ means flesh understood that way (paraphrased). Augustine also stresses spiritual eating by faith."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 6, lect. 8","‘The flesh’ means carnal understanding; Christ’s flesh, united to the Word and the Spirit, profits much (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Jesus also said ‘I am the door’ and ‘I am the vine’. He speaks in metaphors.”","True, and no one walked away from him over ‘I am the door’. The metaphors are obvious and point to a single feature. In John 6 his hearers understood him literally, he repeated himself more strongly, and he let them go. That is not how Jesus handles a metaphor."],
  ["“Eating is believing; 6:35 equates them.”","6:35 uses hunger and thirst as images of faith, and Catholics accept that part of the chapter is about faith. But in 6:52–59 Jesus speaks of his flesh given for the life of the world, of drinking his blood (which has no ‘faith’ parallel), and of future giving. And faith is required to receive the Eucharist; the two are not alternatives."],
  ["“Drinking blood was forbidden by the Law (Leviticus 17:10–14).”","Precisely why the crowd was scandalized, and why Jesus’ insistence is so striking. The Law forbade blood because ‘the life of the flesh is in the blood’ (Leviticus 17:11). Jesus gives his life to us in his blood. The Eucharist is not cannibalism: we receive the living, risen Christ under sacramental signs, not dead flesh."],
  ["“Augustine was a symbolist.”","Augustine uses the language of sign and sacrament and stresses faith, but he also writes that Christ ‘held himself in his own hands’ at the Supper (Exposition on Psalm 33, Sermon 1.10), that no one eats that flesh without first adoring it (Exposition on Psalm 98.9), and that the bread on the altar, sanctified by the word, is the body of Christ (Sermon 227). Sign and reality go together for him."]],
 talk="“Jesus didn’t say ‘my flesh profits nothing’; he said ‘the flesh’, meaning purely human understanding, the way the crowd heard him, as if they’d be cutting him up like meat. His own flesh, he’d just said, is given for the life of the world. And look what happens: many disciples walk away, and Jesus lets them go rather than say ‘I only meant it as a symbol’. The early Christians, like Ignatius of Antioch around 107 AD, said heretics stayed away from the Eucharist ‘because they don’t confess it to be the flesh of our Saviour’.”",
 avoid="Do not imply Catholics think the Eucharist is ‘flesh’ in a crude physical sense (pieces of muscle); Christ is present sacramentally, by way of substance (ST III q.76). Do not say every Protestant is a symbolist; Lutherans affirm the real presence.",
 read=["CCC 1373–1381, 1336","Council of Trent, Session 13 (1551)","Paul VI (1965) Mysterium Fidei","Brant Pitre (2011) Jesus and the Jewish Roots of the Eucharist. New York: Doubleday","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 6, lects. 6–8","Academy module T13m, Lesson 5"])

D('luke',22,19,
 ctx="The Last Supper in Luke 22:14–20 takes place at Passover (22:7–8, 15: ‘With desire I have desired to eat this pasch with you, before I suffer’). ‘And taking bread, he gave thanks, and brake; and gave to them, saying: This is my body, which is given for you. Do this for a commemoration of me. In like manner the chalice also, after he had supped, saying: This is the chalice, the new testament in my blood, which shall be shed for you’ (22:19–20). Paul gives an almost identical account, which he says he ‘received of the Lord’ and ‘delivered’ to the Corinthians (1 Corinthians 11:23–25), written around AD 54.",
 who="The command ‘Do this in remembrance of me’ is read as instituting a memorial meal: Christians remember Christ’s death by sharing bread and wine, which symbolize his body and blood. ‘Remembrance’ means mental recollection, not a sacrifice or a real presence. ‘This is my body’ is a figure of speech, like ‘I am the vine’: ‘this represents my body’.",
 lang="“This is my body” (touto estin to sōma mou). The verb ‘is’ (estin) is the ordinary verb of identity. ‘This’ (touto, neuter) does not match ‘bread’ (artos, masculine): it points to ‘this thing I am giving you’, not ‘this bread’.\n\n“Given” (didomenon) and “shed” (ekchynnomenon) are present participles: ‘being given’, ‘being poured out’. These are sacrificial verbs (cf. Leviticus 4:7, the blood poured out at the altar).\n\n“Commemoration” (anamnēsis). In the Greek Old Testament, anamnēsis is used for sacrificial memorials before God: the showbread offering ‘for a memorial’ (Leviticus 24:7) and sacrifices that ‘may be to you for a remembrance before your God’ (Numbers 10:10). The Passover itself was a ‘memorial’ by which each generation shared in the Exodus (Exodus 12:14; 13:8).\n\n“Do” (poieite) can also have a sacrificial sense, ‘offer’, in the Greek Old Testament (e.g. Exodus 29:38–39, ‘this is what you shall offer (poiēseis) upon the altar’). This is a possible connotation, not a proof.\n\n“The new testament in my blood” echoes Exodus 24:8 (‘This is the blood of the covenant’) and Jeremiah 31:31 (the new covenant).",
 steps=[
  ["The words are plain and repeated four times","Matthew, Mark, Luke and Paul all record ‘This is my body… this is my blood’ (Matthew 26:26–28; Mark 14:22–24; 1 Corinthians 11:24–25). None of them adds an explanation that it is only a figure. In the context of John 6, which the evangelist placed at Passover, the disciples had already heard that they must eat his flesh."],
  ["Unlike ‘I am the vine’, there is no metaphorical frame","‘I am the vine’ is part of an extended parable (John 15:1–8) whose meaning is obvious. At the Supper, Jesus takes a particular object in his hands and identifies it. Paul then treats the identity as real: ‘The chalice of benediction, which we bless, is it not the communion of the blood of Christ? And the bread, which we break, is it not the partaking of the body of the Lord?’ (1 Corinthians 10:16); one who receives unworthily is ‘guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord’ (11:27). You cannot be guilty of the body of someone through misusing a symbol of him."],
  ["‘Remembrance’ is biblical memorial, which makes present","For Israel, remembering the Passover was not daydreaming about history: ‘This day shall be for a memorial to you’ (Exodus 12:14); ‘This is what the Lord did to me when I came forth out of Egypt’ (13:8), said by every generation. The memorial made the saving event present. The Mass is the memorial of Christ’s sacrifice in the same sense: it makes the one sacrifice present (CCC 1362–1366)."],
  ["Sacrificial language throughout","Body ‘given’, blood ‘shed for you’, ‘new testament in my blood’ (covenants were sealed in sacrificial blood, Exodus 24:5–8), at a Passover where the lamb was sacrificed and eaten. Christ offers himself at the Supper, under the signs of bread and wine, and commands the apostles to ‘do this’. That is the institution of both the Eucharist and the apostolic priesthood (Trent, Session 22, ch. 1 and canon 2)."],
  ["The universal witness of the early Church","Ignatius, Justin, Irenaeus, Cyprian, Cyril, Ambrose, Augustine, Chrysostom: all treat the Eucharist as Christ’s body and blood and as a sacrifice. No early Christian writer argues that ‘this is my body’ means ‘this symbolizes my body’ in the Zwinglian sense."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Didache 14 (late 1st century)","Christians gather on the Lord’s day, break bread and give thanks, having confessed their sins, ‘that your sacrifice may be pure’, citing Malachi 1:11."],
  ["Justin Martyr, First Apology 66 (c. 155)","The apostles handed on in the memoirs called Gospels that Jesus took bread and said ‘This do in remembrance of me, this is my body’; this food is the flesh and blood of the incarnate Jesus (paraphrased)."],
  ["Cyprian, Letter 63, 14 (c. 253)","The priest who imitates what Christ did truly fulfils Christ’s office and offers a true and full sacrifice in the Church to God the Father (paraphrased)."],
  ["Ambrose, On the Mysteries 9.52–54 (c. 390)","If the word of Elijah could bring down fire, will not the word of Christ change the nature of the elements? Before consecration it is called bread; after, the body of Christ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 22, canon 2 (1562)","By ‘Do this for a commemoration of me’ Christ instituted the apostles priests and ordained that they and other priests should offer his body and blood."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Jesus was physically present holding the bread. It can’t have been his body.”","God can do what we cannot imagine. The same Jesus walked on water and passed through locked doors (John 20:19). Christ is present in the Eucharist not by occupying space as his natural body does, but sacramentally, by way of substance (ST III q.76 a.5). Catholics hold that at the Supper he gave himself under the signs while visibly present."],
  ["“Aramaic has no verb ‘is’, so Jesus might have said ‘this, my body’ meaning ‘represents’.”","Aramaic can omit the copula, but a juxtaposition ‘this, my body’ is a statement of identity just as strong. And the inspired Greek text has estin. The Church reads what the evangelists wrote."],
  ["“If it were real, Paul would not call it ‘bread’ afterwards (1 Corinthians 11:26).”","See the note on 11:26. Scripture names things by their appearance: angels appearing as men are called ‘men’ (Genesis 18:2; Acts 1:10). After Cana the steward tastes ‘the water made wine’ (John 2:9)."]],
 talk="“Jesus didn’t say ‘this represents my body’; four separate New Testament accounts say ‘This is my body’. Paul says that whoever eats unworthily is guilty of the body and blood of the Lord, which only makes sense if it really is his body. And ‘remembrance’ in the Bible is the word used for the Passover memorial, which made the Exodus present to every generation. That’s what the Mass is: Christ’s one sacrifice made present, and Christ himself given to us.”",
 avoid="Do not rely on the Aramaic argument either way; we have the Greek. Do not imply that the Mass repeats or adds to Calvary (see Hebrews 10:10).",
 read=["CCC 1337–1344, 1362–1381","Council of Trent, Session 13 and Session 22","John Paul II (2003) Ecclesia de Eucharistia, chs. 1–2","Joachim Jeremias (1966) The Eucharistic Words of Jesus. London: SCM (a Lutheran scholar on anamnēsis)","Academy module T13m, Lessons 3 and 5"])

D('hebrews',10,10,
 ctx="Hebrews 9–10 contrasts the old sacrifices, repeated year after year, with Christ’s. ‘In the which will, we are sanctified by the oblation of the body of Jesus Christ once. And every priest indeed standeth daily ministering, and often offering the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins: but this man offering one sacrifice for sins, for ever sitteth on the right hand of God… For by one oblation he hath perfected for ever them that are sanctified’ (10:10–14). ‘Now where there is a remission of these, there is no more an oblation for sin’ (10:18). Yet the same letter says: ‘every high priest is appointed to offer gifts and sacrifices: wherefore it is necessary that he also should have some thing to offer’ (8:3); and ‘we have an altar, whereof they have no power to eat who serve the tabernacle’ (13:10).",
 who="This is the central Protestant objection to the sacrifice of the Mass. Christ was offered ‘once for all’ (ephapax). The Mass, which Catholics call a sacrifice offered daily for the sins of the living and the dead, appears to repeat Calvary, to add to it, or to imply it was not enough. Luther called it ‘the most wicked abuse of all’ (Babylonian Captivity, 1520); the Anglican Thirty-Nine Articles (1571), article 31, called the sacrifices of Masses ‘blasphemous fables and dangerous deceits’.",
 lang="“Once” (ephapax, 10:10; also 7:27; 9:12): once for all, never to be repeated.\n\n“Offering” (prosphora, 10:10, 14, 18): offering, oblation.\n\nIn 8:3 ‘that he also should have some thing to offer’ (ho prosenenkē) is in the context of Christ’s present heavenly ministry (8:1–2). Hebrews also uses the present tense of Christ’s ongoing priestly work: ‘he is able also to save for ever them that come to God by him; always living to make intercession for us’ (7:25); ‘to appear now in the presence of God for us’ (9:24).",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm ‘once for all’","The Council of Trent itself teaches that Christ offered himself ‘once’ on the altar of the Cross (Session 22, ch. 1), and condemns anyone who says the Mass detracts from the sacrifice of the Cross (canon 4). Christ will never die again (Romans 6:9). The Catholic Church does not claim the Mass is a second sacrifice or that Calvary was insufficient."],
  ["The Mass is the same sacrifice, made present, not a new one","Trent: ‘the victim is one and the same, the same now offering by the ministry of priests, who then offered himself on the cross, the manner alone of offering being different’ (Session 22, ch. 2). CCC 1367: ‘The sacrifice of Christ and the sacrifice of the Eucharist are one single sacrifice.’ The Mass re-presents (makes present) the one sacrifice and applies its fruits; it does not add to it."],
  ["Hebrews itself shows that Christ’s offering is ongoing in heaven","Christ ‘continueth for ever’ as priest (7:24); ‘it is necessary that he also should have something to offer’ (8:3); he ‘appears now in the presence of God for us’ (9:24); he ‘always lives to make intercession’ (7:25). John sees in heaven ‘a Lamb standing as it were slain’ (Revelation 5:6). The once-for-all death is eternally present before the Father. The Mass joins the Church on earth to this heavenly offering (Hebrews 12:22–24: ‘you are come to mount Sion… and to Jesus the mediator of the new testament, and to the sprinkling of blood’)."],
  ["The contrast in Hebrews is with repeated Levitical sacrifices","The old priests offered ‘often’ ‘the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins’ (10:11): many different animals, each a new sacrifice, none effective. The Mass is not many victims but the one Victim, Christ, who is the effective sacrifice. Multiplying Masses does not multiply sacrifices any more than multiplying preachings multiplies the gospel."],
  ["Malachi foretold a pure offering among the nations","‘From the rising of the sun even to the going down, my name is great among the Gentiles, and in every place there is sacrifice, and there is offered to my name a clean oblation’ (Malachi 1:11). This cannot be the Cross, offered once in one place. The earliest Christians applied it to the Eucharist (Didache 14; Justin, Dialogue 41; Irenaeus IV.17.5)."],
  ["‘We have an altar’","Hebrews 13:10: Christians have an altar from which those who serve the old tabernacle ‘have no power to eat’. An altar from which one eats implies a sacrifice that is eaten, as in the Passover and peace offerings (1 Corinthians 10:18: ‘they that eat of the sacrifices, are they not partakers of the altar?’)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Didache 14 (late 1st century)","Calls the Sunday Eucharist ‘your sacrifice’ and quotes Malachi 1:11."],
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Hebrews 17.6 (c. 400)","‘Do we not offer every day? We offer indeed, but making a remembrance of his death; and this is one, not many… We offer always the same, not one sheep now and tomorrow another, but always the same thing; so the sacrifice is one’ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Augustine, City of God X.20 (c. 420)","Christ is both priest and oblation, and he wished the daily sacrifice of the Church to be the sacrament of this (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.83 a.1","The Mass is called a sacrifice because it represents Christ’s Passion and makes us partakers of its fruit; quoting Chrysostom: we do not offer another victim, but the same."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 22, chs. 1–2, canons 1–4 (1562)","The Mass is a true and proper sacrifice, the same as the Cross in victim and priest, different only in manner, and it takes nothing away from the Cross."]],
 obj=[
  ["“10:18: where there is forgiveness there is no more offering for sin.”","No more new offering: no further sacrifice beyond Christ’s is needed or possible. The Mass is not a further offering; it is the one offering of Christ, present sacramentally. See the note on Hebrews 10:18."],
  ["“If Christ’s sacrifice is finished, why apply it?”","Because each person must receive what Christ won. Preaching applies it, faith receives it, baptism applies it. Protestants agree that Christ’s finished work must be applied to individuals; Catholics say the Mass is one of the ways Christ himself applies it."],
  ["“Catholics say Christ is ‘immolated’ in the Mass. That means killed again.”","Trent says ‘immolated in an unbloody manner’, i.e. sacramentally, by the separate consecration of bread and wine that signifies his death, while he remains living and glorious (ST III q.76 a.2; Academy module T13m, Lesson 4). He does not suffer or die again."]],
 talk="“Catholics completely agree that Christ was offered once for all and will never die again; the Council of Trent says so. The Mass isn’t another sacrifice; it’s the same one, made present. Hebrews says Christ is still a priest in heaven, ‘appearing before God for us’, and John sees him there as ‘a Lamb standing as though slain’. At Mass, the Church on earth is joined to that one offering. Malachi even foretold a pure offering to God ‘in every place’ among the nations, and the very first Christians, in the Didache, applied that to the Eucharist.”",
 avoid="Do not use language that suggests Christ dies again or suffers at Mass. Do not deny that late medieval abuses (Masses as business) existed; Trent reformed them.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 22 (1562)","CCC 1362–1372","ST III q.83 a.1","Charles Journet (1958) The Mass: The Presence of the Sacrifice of the Cross","Academy module T13m, Lessons 2–4"])

D('1-corinthians',11,27,
 ctx="1 Corinthians 11:17–34 rebukes abuses at the Corinthian Lord’s Supper: divisions, some going hungry while others get drunk (11:21). Paul hands on the words of institution (11:23–25), then: ‘Therefore whosoever shall eat this bread, or drink the chalice of the Lord unworthily, shall be guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord. But let a man prove himself: and so let him eat of that bread, and drink of the chalice. For he that eateth and drinketh unworthily, eateth and drinketh judgment to himself, not discerning the body of the Lord. Therefore are there many infirm and weak among you, and many sleep’ (11:27–30).",
 who="Symbolist Protestants read ‘not discerning the body’ as failing to recognize the Church, the body of Christ (the community), as the Corinthians did by humiliating the poor. Unworthy eating is an ethical failure, a sin against the community and against the memory of Christ’s death, not a sin against a real presence in the bread.",
 lang="“Guilty of” (enochos): liable, answerable for. The same word is used in Matthew 5:21–22 (‘liable to the judgment’) and James 2:10 (‘guilty of all’). To be ‘guilty of the body and blood’ is a phrase for responsibility for someone’s death (compare Hebrews 10:29, ‘trodden under foot the Son of God, and hath esteemed the blood of the testament unclean’).\n\n“Discerning” (diakrinōn): distinguishing, judging rightly.\n\n“Body of the Lord” (to sōma tou kyriou, some manuscripts simply to sōma).",
 steps=[
  ["Guilt for the body and blood implies their presence","One can be ‘guilty of the blood’ of a person only by harming that person. If the bread were only a symbol, unworthy eating would be disrespect toward a symbol, not guilt of the body and blood themselves. Paul treats sacrilege against the Eucharist as a sin against Christ himself."],
  ["The punishment is real and severe","‘Many infirm and weak among you, and many sleep (have died).’ Paul attributes illness and death to receiving unworthily. This is the language of sacrilege, comparable to Uzzah touching the ark (2 Samuel 6:6–7)."],
  ["‘The body’ is linked to ‘this bread’ and ‘the chalice’","The paragraph moves from the words ‘This is my body’ (11:24) to eating ‘this bread’ unworthily (11:27) to ‘not discerning the body’ (11:29). The natural antecedent is the eucharistic body just mentioned. Paul does elsewhere speak of the Church as the body (10:17; 12:27), and Catholic teaching holds both: the one bread makes us one body (10:17). Abusing the poor at the Lord’s Supper is also a failure to discern Christ’s body. But the primary sense in 11:27–29 is the sacrament."],
  ["Paul says the same in chapter 10","‘The chalice of benediction, which we bless, is it not the communion (koinōnia) of the blood of Christ? And the bread, which we break, is it not the partaking of the body of the Lord?’ (10:16). He compares the Eucharist with Jewish and pagan sacrificial meals (10:18–21) and warns against provoking the Lord to jealousy."],
  ["Hence the Church’s discipline","Because the Eucharist is Christ, one who is conscious of grave sin must go to confession before receiving Communion (CCC 1385; CIC 916). This practice comes straight from 11:28, ‘let a man prove himself’."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Didache 14 (late 1st century)","Confess your transgressions before breaking bread, ‘that your sacrifice may be pure’; let no one at variance with his neighbour come until reconciled."],
  ["Origen, Homilies on Exodus 13.3 (c. 240)","You who are used to the divine mysteries know how carefully you guard the body of the Lord lest any crumb fall, and rightly think yourselves guilty if anything falls through negligence (paraphrased)."],
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on 1 Corinthians 24 and 28 (c. 392)","To approach unworthily is like the guilt of those who crucified him (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Paul still calls it ‘bread’ (11:26–28).”","Scripture names it by appearance and as ‘the bread from heaven’ (John 6). See the note on 11:26."],
  ["“‘The body’ is the church community.”","Catholics accept that the social dimension matters; Paul is condemning humiliation of the poor. But ‘guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord’ cannot mean ‘guilty of the community and of the blood’. The blood is not the community."]],
 talk="“Paul says whoever receives unworthily is ‘guilty of the body and blood of the Lord’, and that some Corinthians had become sick and even died because of it. You can’t be guilty of someone’s body and blood by disrespecting a symbol. That only makes sense if it really is Christ. That’s why Catholics go to confession first if they’re conscious of serious sin.”",
 avoid="Do not use this verse to shame people who cannot receive Communion; it is about honesty before God, and confession restores us.",
 read=["CCC 1384–1390","CIC 916","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on 1 Corinthians, ch. 11, lects. 6–7"])

D('1-peter',2,9,
 ctx="1 Peter 2:4–10 describes Christians as ‘living stones built up, a spiritual house, a holy priesthood, to offer up spiritual sacrifices, acceptable to God by Jesus Christ’ (2:5). Then: ‘But you are a chosen generation, a kingly priesthood, a holy nation, a purchased people: that you may declare his virtues, who hath called you out of darkness into his marvellous light’ (2:9). Peter is quoting God’s words to Israel at Sinai: ‘you shall be to me a priestly kingdom, and a holy nation’ (Exodus 19:6).",
 who="This verse is the basis of Luther’s ‘priesthood of all believers’ (To the Christian Nobility, 1520): all baptized Christians are equally priests; there is no special sacramental priesthood; ministers are only functionaries chosen by the congregation to preach and administer the sacraments for the sake of order. A Catholic priesthood that alone can consecrate the Eucharist and absolve sins denies the priesthood of believers.",
 lang="“Kingly priesthood” (basileion hierateuma): the collective noun hierateuma, ‘priestly body’, from Exodus 19:6 in the Greek Old Testament.\n\n“Spiritual sacrifices” (pneumatikas thysias, 2:5): the offering of the whole life (Romans 12:1; Hebrews 13:15–16).\n\nThe New Testament uses hiereus (priest) of Christ and of Old Testament priests; Christian ministers are called presbyteroi (‘elders’, whence the English word ‘priest’) and episkopoi (overseers, bishops). But Paul describes his own ministry with priestly language: ‘ministering (hierourgounta, performing priestly service) the gospel of God, that the oblation of the Gentiles may be made acceptable’ (Romans 15:16).",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics affirm the common priesthood of all the baptized","Vatican II: ‘The baptized, by regeneration and the anointing of the Holy Spirit, are consecrated as a spiritual house and a holy priesthood’ (Lumen Gentium 10, quoting 1 Peter 2). The faithful offer spiritual sacrifices, join in offering the Eucharist, and witness to Christ (CCC 1141, 1268, 1546). This is not a Protestant discovery; it is Catholic doctrine."],
  ["Israel was a priestly nation and still had a ministerial priesthood","Peter quotes Exodus 19:6. At Sinai the whole of Israel was called ‘a priestly kingdom’, and yet in the very next chapters God appoints Aaron and his sons as priests (Exodus 28–29). The two were never in competition: the nation was priestly, and within it was a ministerial priesthood serving the nation."],
  ["Korah’s rebellion used the same argument","Korah, Dathan and Abiram rose against Moses and Aaron saying: ‘all the multitude consisteth of holy ones, and the Lord is among them: Why lift you up yourselves above the people of the Lord?’ (Numbers 16:3). God vindicated Aaron’s priesthood dramatically (16:31–35; 17:1–10). Jude 11 warns Christians against ‘the contradiction of Core (Korah)’."],
  ["The New Testament has ordained ministers with specific powers","The apostles were told ‘do this’ (Luke 22:19) and ‘whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them’ (John 20:23), not the whole crowd. They appointed presbyters by the laying on of hands (Acts 14:22; 1 Timothy 4:14; 5:22; 2 Timothy 1:6; Titus 1:5). Presbyters anoint the sick and pray over them so that sins are forgiven (James 5:14–15). Not every Christian did these things."],
  ["Isaiah foretold priests taken from the Gentiles","‘And I will take of them to be priests, and Levites, saith the Lord’ (Isaiah 66:21), in a prophecy of the nations coming to worship. Paul sees his ministry as priestly service offering the Gentiles to God (Romans 15:16)."],
  ["Two participations in Christ’s one priesthood","The common priesthood and the ministerial priesthood ‘differ essentially and not only in degree’, yet both share in the one priesthood of Christ (LG 10; CCC 1547). The ministerial priest acts in persona Christi capitis, in the person of Christ the head, to build up the common priesthood of all."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Clement of Rome, Letter to the Corinthians 40–44 (c. 96)","Compares the Christian ministry to the ordered priesthood of the Old Testament; the apostles appointed bishops and deacons and provided for succession; it is a sin to remove those who have blamelessly offered the gifts (paraphrased)."],
  ["Ignatius of Antioch, Letter to the Smyrnaeans 8 (c. 107)","Let that be considered a valid Eucharist which is celebrated by the bishop, or by one whom he has appointed (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.63 a.3–6","Baptism and confirmation give a participation in Christ’s priesthood to all; holy orders gives a power to consecrate and to act in Christ’s person."],
  ["Second Vatican Council, Lumen Gentium 10 (1964)","The common priesthood and the ministerial priesthood differ in essence, not only in degree, but are ordered to one another."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The New Testament never calls Christian ministers hiereis (priests).”","True of the word, and significant: the early Christians avoided confusion with the Temple priests while it still stood. But the realities are there: an altar (Hebrews 13:10), a sacrifice (Malachi 1:11; Didache 14), ordained presbyters who forgive sins and anoint. By the late 2nd century, writers such as Tertullian and Hippolytus already use priestly language for bishops (‘high priest’, Apostolic Tradition 3)."],
  ["“If all are priests, why can only an ordained priest consecrate?”","Because Christ gave the command ‘Do this’ to the apostles, and they handed on the ministry by the laying on of hands. The whole people offers the sacrifice with the priest (‘my sacrifice and yours’), but the priest acts in Christ’s person to make it present."]],
 talk="“Peter is quoting what God said to Israel at Sinai: ‘you shall be a kingdom of priests’. Yet in the very same book God set apart Aaron and his sons as priests within that priestly nation. Catholics believe both: all the baptized share Christ’s priesthood, and some are ordained to serve the rest, the way the apostles were told ‘do this in memory of me’ and ‘whose sins you forgive are forgiven’. Korah actually used your argument against Aaron in Numbers 16, and it didn’t go well for him.”",
 avoid="Do not say lay people are not priests at all; that contradicts Vatican II. Use the Korah example gently; it can sound like a threat.",
 read=["Lumen Gentium 10–11 (1964)","CCC 1539–1553","1 Clement 40–44","ST III q.63; Suppl. qq.34–40","Academy module T13m, Lesson 7"])

D('mark',2,7,
 ctx="Mark 2:1–12. A paralytic is let down through the roof. ‘And when Jesus had seen their faith, he saith to the sick of the palsy: Son, thy sins are forgiven thee. And there were some of the scribes sitting there, and thinking in their hearts: Why doth this man speak thus? he blasphemeth. Who can forgive sins, but God only?’ (2:5–7). Jesus answers: ‘that you may know that the Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins, (he saith to the sick of the palsy,) I say to thee: Arise, take up thy bed, and go into thy house’ (2:10–11). Matthew’s version ends: ‘the multitude seeing it, feared, and glorified God that gave such power to men’ (Matthew 9:8).",
 who="Used against confession to a priest: only God can forgive sins; a priest who says ‘I absolve you’ claims what belongs to God alone. Christians should confess directly to God.",
 lang="“Power” (exousia): authority. ‘The Son of man hath power on earth to forgive sins’ stresses that this divine authority is exercised on earth, through a human nature. Matthew 9:8: ‘glorified God that gave such power (exousian) to men (tois anthrōpois)’, plural.\n\nJohn 20:23: ‘Whose sins you shall forgive (aphēte), they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain (kratēte), they are retained.’ Aphiēmi is the same verb used in Mark 2:5.",
 steps=[
  ["The scribes’ principle was true, and their conclusion was wrong","Only God can forgive sins by his own authority. The scribes were right about that. Their error was to deny that God was acting in Jesus. Catholics agree that only God forgives sins; the question is whether God chooses to forgive through human ministers. The scribes in effect said ‘God cannot do it through a man’. Jesus proved them wrong."],
  ["Jesus exercised divine forgiveness on earth, through human nature","‘The Son of man hath power on earth.’ Jesus forgave in his humanity, as the instrument of his divinity (ST III q.13 a.2; q.84 a.3). This sets the pattern: God forgives through visible, human means."],
  ["The crowd praised God for giving such power ‘to men’","Matthew 9:8 uses the plural. Matthew wrote for a Church that already knew Christ had shared this authority (Matthew 16:19; 18:18)."],
  ["The risen Christ gave the apostles power to forgive and retain sins","‘As the Father hath sent me, I also send you. When he had said this, he breathed on them; and he said to them: Receive ye the Holy Ghost. Whose sins you shall forgive, they are forgiven them; and whose sins you shall retain, they are retained’ (John 20:21–23). To ‘retain’ requires knowing the sins, which requires confession. Paul: God ‘hath given to us the ministry of reconciliation… we are therefore ambassadors for Christ’ (2 Corinthians 5:18–20)."],
  ["The early Church practised confession and absolution","James 5:14–16: the presbyters pray over the sick, ‘and if he be in sins, they shall be forgiven him. Confess therefore your sins one to another.’ Acts 19:18: ‘many of them that believed, came confessing, and declaring their deeds’. The Didache (4.14; 14.1) commands confession in church. Public penance with episcopal reconciliation is attested from the 2nd century (Hermas; Tertullian, On Penance 9; Cyprian, On the Lapsed 28–29); private confession spread from the 6th century."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Cyprian, On the Lapsed 29 (c. 251)","Let each confess his sin while still in this world, while his confession can be received and the satisfaction and remission made through the priests is pleasing to the Lord (paraphrased)."],
  ["Ambrose, On Penance I.2.7 (c. 390)","Against the Novatians: Christ gave the power of binding and loosing to the priests; why do you claim to honour God by denying what he gave (paraphrased)?"],
  ["John Chrysostom, On the Priesthood III.5 (c. 386)","Priests have received an authority God has given neither to angels nor archangels: what they bind on earth is bound in heaven (paraphrased)."],
  ["Council of Trent, Session 14, ch. 6 and canon 3 (1551)","John 20:22–23 is to be understood of the power to forgive and retain sins in the sacrament of penance."]],
 obj=[
  ["“John 20:23 means preaching the gospel: whoever believes is forgiven.”","Jesus breathes on the apostles in a solemn commissioning, gives them the Spirit for this act, and distinguishes forgiving from retaining. Preaching does not ‘retain’ specific people’s sins. The Church has always read it of the power of the keys (Trent, Session 14, canon 3)."],
  ["“I can confess to God directly.”","Yes, and Catholics do so every day (the Confiteor, the Our Father, an act of contrition). Perfect contrition with the intention to confess restores grace even before confession (CCC 1452). But Christ gave a sacrament where his forgiveness is spoken in audible words by his minister, and the Church obliges confession of grave sins in it (CCC 1456–1457)."]],
 talk="“The scribes were right that only God can forgive sins, and wrong to think God couldn’t do it through a man. Jesus proved he could forgive on earth, and Matthew says the crowd praised God for giving such authority ‘to men’. Then on Easter night Jesus breathed on the apostles and said, ‘Whose sins you forgive are forgiven; whose sins you retain are retained.’ When a priest absolves, it’s God forgiving, through the ministry Christ set up.”",
 avoid="Do not imply the priest forgives by his own power; he is Christ’s minister (CCC 1441–1442).",
 read=["CCC 1440–1449, 1461–1467","Council of Trent, Session 14 (1551)","Scott Hahn (2003) Lord, Have Mercy: The Healing Power of Confession. New York: Doubleday"])

# ---------------- New entries ----------------
add('hebrews',10,18,'Hebrews 10:18','"There is no more an oblation for sin"',
 'Since Christ\'s sacrifice has obtained forgiveness, there can be no further offering for sin. The Mass, offered "for sins", is therefore excluded.',
 ['The verse rules out any further, different sacrifice. The Mass is not a different sacrifice but the one sacrifice of Christ, made present (Trent, Session 22, ch. 2).',
  'Hebrews itself says Christ continues his priestly offering in heaven (8:3; 9:24) and that Christians "have an altar" (13:10).',
  'The Mass does not obtain a new forgiveness beyond Calvary; it applies the forgiveness won on Calvary.',
  'The context contrasts the Levitical sacrifices, repeated because they never forgave sin, with Christ\'s, which did.'],
 'No new offering beyond Christ\'s; the Mass is that offering, present for us.',
 'Trent, Session 22, chs. 1–2, canon 4; CCC 1366–1367',
 tags=('mass',))
D('hebrews',10,18,
 ctx="Hebrews 10:15–18 quotes Jeremiah’s new-covenant prophecy (‘their sins and iniquities I will remember no more’, Jeremiah 31:34) and concludes: ‘Now where there is a remission of these, there is no more an oblation for sin’ (10:18). The next verses invite Christians to draw near ‘into the holies by the blood of Christ’ (10:19) and warn against deliberate sin after receiving the truth: ‘there is now left no sacrifice for sins’ (10:26).",
 who="Combined with 10:10–14, this verse is used to say that any further ‘offering for sin’, especially a Mass described as ‘propitiatory’ for the living and the dead (Trent, Session 22, canon 3), contradicts Scripture.",
 lang="“Oblation” (prosphora): offering. “No more” (ouketi): no longer, i.e. no further offering of a different kind is needed, as the Levitical ones were.",
 steps=[
  ["‘No more offering’ means no other offering","The author has just said the old sacrifices were repeated because they did not remove sin (10:1–4, 11). Once forgiveness is accomplished by Christ, there is no place for any other sacrifice for sin. Catholics agree: there is no sacrifice other than Christ’s."],
  ["10:26 confirms the meaning","‘If we sin wilfully after having the knowledge of the truth, there is now left no sacrifice for sins.’ Whoever rejects Christ’s sacrifice has no other to turn to. The point is the uniqueness of Christ’s sacrifice, not the absence of any sacramental making-present of it."],
  ["The Mass is the one offering","Trent: the Mass is offered to ‘represent’ the bloody sacrifice of the Cross, keep its memory to the end of the world and ‘apply its saving virtue to the remission of those sins which we daily commit’ (Session 22, ch. 1). It obtains nothing that Calvary did not already merit. It is the channel, not a new source."],
  ["Propitiatory because it is Calvary present","The Mass is called propitiatory (Trent, canon 3) because the same propitiation that Christ offered (1 John 2:2) is present and offered. It turns away God’s just anger not by a new merit but by presenting the one perfect Victim."]],
 fathers=[
  ["John Chrysostom, Homilies on Hebrews 17.6","We offer the same sacrifice, not another; or rather we make a remembrance of the sacrifice (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Hebrews, ch. 10, lect. 1–2","The Church’s offering is not distinct from Christ’s, but a commemoration of it (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Then why must Masses be offered again and again?”","Not because Calvary is incomplete, but because each generation, each day, each soul needs its fruits applied, as the gospel is preached again and again although Christ’s word was spoken once."]],
 talk="“Hebrews means there’s no other sacrifice besides Christ’s; the Old Testament sacrifices are over. Catholics agree. The Mass isn’t another sacrifice; it’s Christ’s one sacrifice made present, so that its forgiveness reaches us today. It doesn’t add anything to Calvary; it delivers Calvary.”",
 avoid="Avoid saying the Mass ‘earns’ new forgiveness; say it applies Christ’s.",
 read=["Council of Trent, Session 22","CCC 1366–1367","Academy module T13m, Lesson 3"])

add('john',19,30,'John 19:30','"It is consummated" (It is finished)',
 'Jesus declared the work of salvation finished on the Cross. So nothing can be added: no Mass, no purgatory, no penance, no merit.',
 ['Catholics agree that Christ\'s sacrifice was completed on the Cross and needs no addition.',
  'John\'s word (tetelestai) means the work given him by the Father, the sacrifice and the fulfilment of Scripture, is accomplished. It does not mean that nothing more happens: the Resurrection, Ascension and Pentecost follow, and Paul says Christ "rose again for our justification" (Romans 4:25).',
  'The finished work must still be applied to each person through faith, baptism, the Eucharist and penance. Protestants too believe it must be applied, through faith.',
  'Paul even says: "I fill up those things that are wanting of the sufferings of Christ, in my flesh, for his body, which is the church" (Colossians 1:24).'],
 'The sacrifice is finished; its fruits are applied to every generation until the end of time.',
 'CCC 2606, 1366, 618; Colossians 1:24',
 tags=('mass','purgatory'))
D('john',19,30,
 ctx="John 19:28–30: ‘Afterwards, Jesus knowing that all things were now accomplished, that the scripture might be fulfilled, said: I thirst. Now there was a vessel set there full of vinegar. And they, putting a sponge full of vinegar about hyssop, put it to his mouth. Jesus therefore, when he had taken the vinegar, said: It is consummated. And bowing his head, he gave up the ghost.’ John then reports the piercing of his side, ‘and immediately there came out blood and water’ (19:34).",
 who="‘It is finished’ (tetelestai) is said to be a commercial term for ‘paid in full’, written on receipts: Christ paid the whole debt of sin, so purgatory (which implies a remaining debt of temporal punishment), indulgences, penance and the Mass (an ongoing sacrifice) all deny that the debt was paid in full.",
 lang="Tetelestai: perfect passive of teleō, ‘to finish, complete, accomplish, fulfil’. John uses the same verb two verses earlier: ‘knowing that all things were now accomplished (tetelestai)’ (19:28), linked to fulfilling Scripture. The ‘paid in full’ meaning rests on its use in some papyri receipts (where it can mark a bill paid); it is a possible overtone, but John’s own usage points to the completion of the work the Father gave him (4:34; 17:4: ‘I have finished (teleiōsas) the work which thou gavest me to do’).",
 steps=[
  ["Catholics agree Christ’s work of redemption was accomplished","Christ’s Passion merited all grace and made superabundant satisfaction for all sins (ST III q.48 a.2; CCC 616–617). There is no deficiency in Christ’s sacrifice that we could make up."],
  ["‘Finished’ refers to the work the Father gave him and the fulfilment of Scripture","John links tetelestai to Scripture being fulfilled (19:28) and to Jesus’ mission (17:4). The Passover Lamb has been sacrificed. Not everything in salvation history ended at that moment: the Resurrection (‘he rose again for our justification’, Romans 4:25), the Ascension, the sending of the Spirit and Christ’s heavenly intercession (Hebrews 7:25) were still to come."],
  ["Everyone agrees the finished work must be applied","A Protestant who says ‘it is finished’ still believes a person is not saved until he believes. Faith is the means of applying Christ’s finished work. Catholics say Christ also appointed sacraments as means of applying it: baptism (Acts 2:38), the Eucharist (John 6:54), absolution (John 20:23). Applying the work does not add to it."],
  ["Purgatory and penance are applications, not additions","Temporal punishment and purification remain after forgiveness because our sins leave disorder in us, as David was forgiven yet suffered consequences (2 Samuel 12:13–14). Purgatory is Christ’s finished work completing our healing; its power comes entirely from his Cross (CCC 1030–1032, 1472–1473). Paul himself speaks of ‘filling up what is wanting of the sufferings of Christ for his body’ (Colossians 1:24), not because Christ’s sufferings lacked anything, but because Christ wills his members to share in them."],
  ["The Mass makes the finished sacrifice present","The Mass offers no new work; it presents the finished one (see Hebrews 10:10). It is like a fountain that flows from a finished spring."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Tractates on John 119.6 (c. 420)","‘It is finished’: all that prophecy foretold of him has been accomplished; he who had power to lay down his life laid it down (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 19, lect. 5","‘It is consummated’: the Passion fulfilled the prophecies, the figures of the Law, and the work of our redemption (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“‘Paid in full’ means no temporal punishment remains.”","The eternal debt of sin was paid, and nothing we do adds to Christ’s satisfaction. But Scripture shows forgiven people undergoing discipline and purification (Hebrews 12:6–11; 1 Corinthians 3:15). Even Protestants accept that forgiven Christians still suffer the consequences of sin in this life."]],
 talk="“Jesus finished the work the Father gave him: the sacrifice for sin is complete and perfect, and nothing can be added to it. Catholics believe that. But it still has to reach each person. Even Protestants say you’re not saved until you believe. Catholics just add that Christ also gave sacraments to apply it. The Mass, confession and even purgatory don’t add to the Cross; they’re the Cross reaching us.”",
 avoid="Do not dispute the ‘paid in full’ overtone aggressively; it is a possible nuance. Make the main point: completion of the sacrifice, application to us.",
 read=["CCC 613–618, 1030–1032, 1471–1473","ST III q.48","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 19"])

add('hebrews',7,24,'Hebrews 7:24','"He hath an everlasting priesthood"',
 'Christ\'s priesthood is "unchangeable" (or "untransferable", aparabatos): it cannot pass to anyone else. So there can be no Catholic priests succeeding him.',
 ['Catholic priests do not succeed Christ or replace him. Christ remains the one high priest; ordained priests share in his priesthood as his ministers (CCC 1545).',
  'The contrast in Hebrews is with Levitical priests who died and were replaced (7:23). Christ never dies, so he has no successor.',
  'Thomas Aquinas: "Christ alone is the true priest; the others are only his ministers."',
  'The same Christ who holds the eternal priesthood said to the apostles "Do this" and "whose sins you forgive".'],
 'One eternal priest, Christ; many ministers acting in his person, not in his place.',
 'CCC 1544–1547; ST III q.22',
 tags=('priesthood',))
D('hebrews',7,24,
 ctx="Hebrews 7 argues from Psalm 109:4 (‘Thou art a priest for ever according to the order of Melchisedech’) that Christ’s priesthood surpasses the Levitical one. ‘And the others indeed were made many priests, because by reason of death they were not suffered to continue: but this, for that he continueth for ever, hath an everlasting priesthood: whereby he is able also to save for ever them that come to God by him; always living to make intercession for us’ (7:23–25).",
 who="Since Christ’s priesthood is ‘untransferable’, there can be no human priests who carry on a priestly ministry. Catholic priests, especially as those who ‘offer sacrifice’, usurp Christ’s office.",
 lang="“Everlasting” (aparabatos) occurs only here in the New Testament. Its meaning is debated: ‘permanent, unchangeable’ (the usual sense in Greek legal texts) or ‘not passing to another, without successor’. Either way, the contrast is with priests who die and are replaced (7:23).",
 steps=[
  ["Christ has no successor, and Catholics say none","Catholic priests are not Christ’s successors. Christ is alive and is ‘the one priest’. The Church says the ministerial priest acts in persona Christi: Christ himself acts through him (CCC 1548). A minister is not a replacement."],
  ["Participation, not substitution","‘The redemptive sacrifice of Christ is unique… it is made present in the Eucharistic sacrifice of the Church. The same is true of the one priesthood of Christ; it is made present through the ministerial priesthood without diminishing the uniqueness of Christ’s priesthood’ (CCC 1545). As Christ is the one Shepherd and yet appoints shepherds (John 21:16; 1 Peter 5:1–4, where Peter calls Christ ‘the prince of pastors’)."],
  ["Thomas’ formula","‘Christ alone is the true priest; the others are his ministers’ (Thomas, Commentary on Hebrews, ch. 7, lect. 4, paraphrased). Christ is the principal agent of every sacrament; the priest is his living instrument (ST III q.64 a.1, 5; q.82 a.1)."],
  ["The apostles received a share in Christ’s mission","‘As the Father hath sent me, I also send you’ (John 20:21). ‘Do this for a commemoration of me’ (Luke 22:19). ‘He that heareth you heareth me’ (Luke 10:16). Paul describes his ministry as priestly service (Romans 15:16)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Augustine, Tractates on John 6.7 (c. 407)","When Peter baptizes, it is Christ who baptizes; when Judas baptizes, it is Christ who baptizes (paraphrased): the minister is Christ’s instrument."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.22 a.4; q.82 a.1 ad 4","Christ is the principal priest; the priest consecrates in the person of Christ."]],
 obj=[
  ["“If Christ does everything, why need a priest?”","Because Christ chose to act visibly through human ministers, as he chose to save through his visible human nature. ‘How shall they preach unless they be sent?’ (Romans 10:15)."]],
 talk="“Exactly: Christ has no successor because he never dies. Catholic priests don’t replace him; they’re his ministers, and Christ acts through them. When a priest says ‘This is my body’, he’s speaking in Christ’s person, not his own. It’s one priesthood, Christ’s, shared out, just as there’s one Good Shepherd who still told Peter ‘feed my sheep’.”",
 avoid="Do not speak of priests as ‘other Christs’ in a way that suggests rivalry; the phrase (alter Christus) is traditional but must be explained as configuration to Christ, not substitution.",
 read=["CCC 1544–1553","ST III q.22; q.82","Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on Hebrews, ch. 7"])

add('matthew',26,29,'Matthew 26:29','"I will not drink… of this fruit of the vine"',
 'After the words of consecration Jesus still calls the cup "this fruit of the vine". So it was still wine, and transubstantiation is false.',
 ['In Luke\'s fuller account, Jesus says "I will not drink of the fruit of the vine" (Luke 22:18) over a cup before the consecration of the chalice (22:20). The evangelists do not all keep the same order.',
  'Even after consecration, the appearances of wine remain, and Scripture names things by how they appear: the steward tastes "the water made wine" (John 2:9).',
  'Jesus has just said "This is my blood of the new testament, which shall be shed for many unto remission of sins" (26:28). That statement is the one to interpret plainly.',
  'The phrase also looks forward to the kingdom ("until that day when I shall drink it with you new"), not backward to the chemistry of the cup.'],
 'Christ called the cup his blood; the words "fruit of the vine" fit its appearance and look ahead to the heavenly banquet.',
 'CCC 1374–1377; ST III q.75 a.2 ad 1',
 disp='d-john6',tags=('eucharist',))
D('matthew',26,29,
 ctx="Matthew 26:26–29: ‘And whilst they were at supper, Jesus took bread, and blessed, and broke: and gave to his disciples, and said: Take ye, and eat. This is my body. And taking the chalice, he gave thanks, and gave to them, saying: Drink ye all of this. For this is my blood of the new testament, which shall be shed for many unto remission of sins. And I say to you, I will not drink from henceforth of this fruit of the vine, until that day when I shall drink it with you new in the kingdom of my Father.’ Luke’s account has two cups: one before the bread, with the saying ‘I will not drink of the fruit of the vine, till the kingdom of God come’ (22:17–18), and the eucharistic chalice after supper (22:20).",
 who="An argument popular since the Reformation: Jesus himself, after consecrating, calls the contents ‘fruit of the vine’. If it had become his blood, he would not call it wine.",
 lang="“Fruit of the vine” (gennēmatos tēs ampelou) is the Jewish blessing over wine (‘Blessed are you, Lord… who creates the fruit of the vine’). Passover meals included several cups of wine, which explains Luke’s two cups.",
 steps=[
  ["The order differs between Gospels","Luke places the ‘fruit of the vine’ saying over an earlier Passover cup (22:17–18), before the institution of the Eucharist (22:19–20). Matthew and Mark place it after. The evangelists group sayings; we cannot build a doctrine on the sequence in one Gospel when another places the words before consecration."],
  ["Things are named by their appearance","After consecration the species (appearances) of wine remain: colour, taste, smell. Scripture often names things by how they appear: angels are called ‘men’ (Genesis 18:2; Mark 16:5); the steward tastes ‘the water made wine’ (John 2:9); Aaron’s rod that became a serpent is still called a ‘rod’ when it swallows the others (Exodus 7:12). Thomas answers exactly this objection (ST III q.75 a.2 ad 1, on ‘bread’ in 1 Corinthians 11)."],
  ["The decisive words are ‘This is my blood’","Jesus identifies the cup as his blood of the covenant, ‘shed… unto remission of sins’. Symbolic blood does not remit sins. The eschatological saying that follows does not cancel this identification."],
  ["The saying points to the kingdom","The emphasis is ‘until that day… new in the kingdom of my Father’: a vow that his next festive cup will be in the kingdom, which the Passion and Resurrection inaugurate. The Fathers connected it with his eating and drinking with the disciples after the Resurrection (Acts 10:41) and with the heavenly banquet."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Cyril of Jerusalem, Mystagogical Catecheses 4.2 (c. 350)","He once turned water into wine at Cana; is he not worthy of belief when he changes wine into blood (paraphrased)?"],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.75 a.2 ad 1; q.81 a.1","Christ drank his own blood at the Supper, according to some Fathers; things keep the names of their appearances."]],
 obj=[
  ["“This proves the substance of wine remained.”","It proves only that Jesus used the name the cup’s appearance suggested, if the saying in Matthew does follow the consecration. Lutherans, who believe the real presence, faced this argument too and did not find it decisive."]],
 talk="“Luke actually puts that saying before the cup is consecrated, so you can’t rely on the order. And even after consecration it still looks and tastes like wine, and the Bible names things by how they appear: after Cana, the steward tastes ‘the water made wine’. The key words are ‘This is my blood of the covenant, poured out for the forgiveness of sins’. Symbolic blood can’t forgive sins.”",
 avoid="Do not claim certainty about which Passover cup is meant in each Gospel; scholars disagree.",
 read=["CCC 1374–1377","ST III q.75 a.2","Brant Pitre (2011) Jesus and the Jewish Roots of the Eucharist, chs. 6–7"])

add('1-corinthians',11,26,'1 Corinthians 11:26','"As often as you shall eat this bread"',
 'Paul still calls it "bread" after consecration and says it "shews the death of the Lord": a symbolic proclamation, not a real presence or a sacrifice.',
 ['In the next verse Paul says unworthy eating makes one "guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord" (11:27).',
  'Christ called himself "the living bread" (John 6:51–52); "bread" is a name for the Eucharist, not a denial of the change.',
  'Appearances remain after consecration, and Scripture names things by appearance (Genesis 18:2; John 2:9).',
  'To "shew the death of the Lord" (katangellete, "proclaim") fits the Mass perfectly: the sacrament makes present and proclaims the sacrifice of Calvary until he comes.'],
 'The Eucharist is called bread because it looks like bread and because it is the true bread from heaven; and it proclaims his death because it makes his sacrifice present.',
 'CCC 1329, 1373–1377; ST III q.75 a.2 ad 1',
 disp='d-john6',tags=('eucharist','mass'))
D('1-corinthians',11,26,
 ctx="After handing on the words of institution (11:23–25), Paul says: ‘For as often as you shall eat this bread, and drink the chalice, you shall shew the death of the Lord, until he come’ (11:26). Then: ‘Therefore whosoever shall eat this bread, or drink the chalice of the Lord unworthily, shall be guilty of the body and of the blood of the Lord’ (11:27).",
 who="The proclamation reading: the Lord’s Supper is a dramatic proclamation of Christ’s death, an enacted sermon; Paul’s calling it ‘bread’ shows the element remains bread. Some Reformed theologians speak of a spiritual presence received by faith while the bread remains bread.",
 lang="“Shew” (katangellete) means ‘announce, proclaim’. It is used of preaching (1 Corinthians 9:14). Present tense: every celebration proclaims.\n\n“This bread” (ton arton touton): ‘this’ refers back to the bread over which Christ said ‘This is my body’ (11:24).",
 steps=[
  ["‘Bread’ is a scriptural name for the Eucharist","Jesus: ‘I am the living bread which came down from heaven… the bread that I will give, is my flesh’ (John 6:51–52). The Eucharist is called ‘bread’ because it is the true bread of life and because it keeps the appearances of bread. The Church still calls it ‘the Bread of Life’ in the liturgy."],
  ["Paul immediately says ‘guilty of the body and blood’","Read 11:26 with 11:27–29 (see that note). The same ‘bread’ eaten unworthily brings guilt of the Lord’s body and blood."],
  ["Proclaiming his death is what the Mass does","The Mass is the memorial that makes present Christ’s death. It proclaims it not as a sermon only but as an action: the separate consecration of body and blood sacramentally shows forth his death (ST III q.76 a.2 ad 1; Academy T13m, Lesson 4). The Church at every Mass acclaims: ‘We proclaim your Death, O Lord… until you come again’."],
  ["‘Until he come’ does not mean he is absent","Christ is present in the Eucharist sacramentally, not in the manner of his visible coming in glory. The real presence and the expectation of his return belong together (CCC 1344, 1402–1405)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST III q.75 a.2 ad 1","The Apostle calls it bread after consecration because it has the appearance of bread, and because it is the bread from heaven (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on 1 Corinthians, ch. 11, lect. 6","We proclaim the Lord’s death by eating, because the sacrament represents his Passion (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The plainest reading is that bread stays bread.”","The plainest reading of ‘This is my body’ two verses earlier is that it is his body. Both must be read together, and the early Church read them as Catholics do."]],
 talk="“Paul calls it bread because it still looks like bread, and because Jesus is ‘the living bread from heaven’. But in the very next verse he says that whoever eats it unworthily is guilty of the body and blood of the Lord. And ‘proclaiming his death’ is exactly what the Mass does: it makes his sacrifice present until he comes back.”",
 avoid="Do not deny that the Eucharist is a proclamation; it is, and more.",
 read=["CCC 1329–1332, 1362–1381","ST III q.75 a.2; q.76 a.2"])

add('hebrews',4,16,'Hebrews 4:16','"Let us go… with confidence to the throne of grace"',
 'Every Christian can approach God directly through Christ, so there is no need for priests, confession, saints or Mary as go-betweens.',
 ['Catholics approach God directly every day, in personal prayer and in the liturgy. The Our Father is addressed straight to the Father.',
  'Direct access to God and asking others to pray for us are not alternatives. Paul, who had direct access, constantly asked others to pray for him (Romans 15:30; Ephesians 6:19).',
  'Christ, who gives us direct access, also gave the apostles authority to forgive sins (John 20:23) and told the sick to call the presbyters (James 5:14).',
  'In the liturgy, the whole Church approaches the throne together with angels and saints (Hebrews 12:22–24).'],
 'We approach the throne of grace with confidence, as a family that prays together, using the means Christ gave.',
 'CCC 2559–2565, 2634–2636, 2683',
 tags=('prayer','saints','confession'))
D('hebrews',4,16,
 ctx="Hebrews 4:14–16: ‘Having therefore a great high priest that hath passed into the heavens, Jesus the Son of God: let us hold fast our confession. For we have not a high priest, who can not have compassion on our infirmities: but one tempted in all things like as we are, without sin. Let us go therefore with confidence to the throne of grace: that we may obtain mercy, and find grace in seasonable aid.’",
 who="Used against the ‘mediation’ of priests, saints and Mary: because of Christ every believer has direct access to God, so Catholic practices that route prayer and forgiveness through others obscure that access.",
 lang="“Confidence” (parrēsia): boldness, freedom of speech. “Let us go” (proserchōmetha): let us keep approaching, a word used for approaching God in worship and sacrifice (10:22; 12:18, 22).",
 steps=[
  ["Catholic prayer is constantly direct","The Mass, the Divine Office, the Our Father and most Catholic prayers are addressed directly to God the Father, through Christ, in the Holy Spirit. The Catechism describes prayer as a personal relationship with God (CCC 2558–2565)."],
  ["Intercession of others is biblical alongside direct access","Paul has direct access and still says: ‘I beseech you therefore, brethren… that you help me in your prayers for me to God’ (Romans 15:30). James: ‘pray one for another, that you may be saved. For the continual prayer of a just man availeth much’ (5:16). Asking a friend, or a saint in heaven, to pray with us is not a detour around Christ; it is the Body of Christ praying together."],
  ["The plural ‘let us’ is liturgical","Hebrews envisions the community approaching together (10:19–25, ‘not forsaking our assembly’), and in 12:22–24 describes it: ‘you are come to mount Sion… and to the company of many thousands of angels, and to the church of the firstborn… and to the spirits of the just made perfect’. The saints are part of the worshipping assembly."],
  ["Christ himself gave sacramental means","The same high priest instituted confession (John 20:23) and the anointing of the sick through presbyters (James 5:14–15). Using the means Christ gave is approaching him, not avoiding him."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Origen, On Prayer 11 (c. 233)","Not only the High Priest prays with those who pray, but also the angels and the souls of the saints who have fallen asleep (paraphrased)."],
  ["CCC 2683","The witnesses who have gone before us into the kingdom share in the living tradition of prayer; they contemplate God, praise him and constantly care for those on earth."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Why ask a saint when you can ask God?”","Why ask your pastor or your mother to pray for you, when you can ask God? Because God wills us to love and help one another, and ‘the prayer of a just man availeth much’. The saints are the most just people there are."]],
 talk="“Catholics go straight to God all the time; the Mass and the Our Father are addressed directly to the Father. But going directly to God doesn’t stop us asking others to pray too. Paul had direct access and still begged churches to pray for him. Hebrews 12 says when we worship we’re joined by angels and ‘the spirits of the just made perfect’. We approach the throne as a family.”",
 avoid="Do not imply that Catholics must go through saints or Mary to reach God; they need not.",
 read=["CCC 2558–2565, 2634–2636, 2683–2684","Hebrews 12:18–24"])

add('1-john',1,9,'1 John 1:9','"If we confess our sins, he is faithful… to forgive"',
 'We confess our sins to God and he forgives us. There is no mention of a priest, so confession to a priest is unnecessary.',
 ['The verse says we must confess, but not to whom, or how. Scripture elsewhere specifies: "Confess your sins one to another" (James 5:16), in the context of calling the presbyters (5:14).',
  'Jesus gave the apostles authority to forgive and retain sins (John 20:23), which requires hearing them confessed.',
  'In the early Church, confession was public and made in church (Didache 4.14; Acts 19:18).',
  'Catholics do confess to God; in the sacrament they confess to God through the minister Christ appointed.'],
 'God is faithful to forgive the sins we confess, and Christ appointed a ministry through which that forgiveness is spoken.',
 'CCC 1440–1449, 1455–1458',
 tags=('confession',))
D('1-john',1,9,
 ctx="1 John 1:5–2:2: ‘If we walk in the light, as he also is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin. If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves… If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just, to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all iniquity… My little children, these things I write to you, that you may not sin. But if any man sin, we have an advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the just: and he is the propitiation for our sins’ (1:7–2:2).",
 who="Private confession of sins directly to God is all Scripture requires. Auricular confession to a priest is a later human invention (often dated to the Fourth Lateran Council, 1215, which required annual confession).",
 lang="“Confess” (homologōmen): to acknowledge, agree, say the same thing; often used of public acknowledgement (Matthew 10:32; Romans 10:9). It is plural: ‘if we confess’, written to a community.",
 steps=[
  ["The verse commands confession without specifying the form","It establishes that confession leads to forgiveness. It does not exclude confession to a minister; in fact the word typically means an open acknowledgement."],
  ["Scripture elsewhere specifies a human hearer","James 5:16: ‘Confess therefore your sins one to another’, right after telling the sick to call ‘the priests (presbyterous) of the church’ (5:14). Acts 19:18: ‘many of them that believed came confessing (exomologoumenoi) and declaring their deeds’. Matthew 3:6: the people were baptized by John ‘confessing their sins’."],
  ["Christ’s commission requires confession","John 20:23: the apostles forgive or retain sins. They cannot judge which sins to retain without hearing them. Trent concluded that confession of grave sins to a priest is of divine law (Session 14, ch. 5, canon 7)."],
  ["The 1215 council did not invent confession","Lateran IV required confession at least once a year: a minimum frequency for an existing practice. Confession and absolution are attested long before: Didache 4.14 (‘in the church thou shalt confess thy transgressions’), Origen (Homilies on Leviticus 2.4, c. 245: the sinner ‘does not shrink from declaring his sin to a priest of the Lord’), Cyprian (On the Lapsed 28), Basil (Short Rules 288)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Didache 4.14 (late 1st century)","‘In the church thou shalt acknowledge thy transgressions, and thou shalt not come to thy prayer with an evil conscience.’"],
  ["Origen, Homilies on Leviticus 2.4 (c. 245)","Among the ways of remission of sins: the hard and laborious way of penance, when the sinner does not shrink from declaring his sin to the priest of the Lord and seeking remedy (paraphrased)."],
  ["Basil the Great, Short Rules 288 (c. 370)","Sins must be confessed to those entrusted with the dispensation of the mysteries of God (paraphrased)."]],
 obj=[
  ["“The Bible never says ‘confess to a priest’.”","James 5:14–16 links confession with calling the presbyters; John 20:23 implies hearing sins; the early Church practised it. Scripture never says ‘confess only privately to God’ either."]],
 talk="“1 John 1:9 says we have to confess, but not to whom. James tells the sick to call the presbyters and then says ‘confess your sins to one another’. Jesus told the apostles ‘whose sins you forgive are forgiven, whose sins you retain are retained’, and they can’t retain sins they’ve never heard. The Didache, from the first century, already says ‘confess your sins in church’. So confession to a priest is confessing to God, the way Christ arranged.”",
 avoid="Do not imply private prayer of repentance is useless; it is good and necessary. Perfect contrition even restores grace before confession (CCC 1452).",
 read=["CCC 1440–1460","Council of Trent, Session 14, chs. 5–6","Didache 4, 14"])

add('john',4,24,'John 4:24','"Adore him in spirit and in truth"',
 'True worship is inward and spiritual. Catholic liturgy, with its altars, vestments, candles, incense, images and set rituals, is the kind of external worship Jesus replaced.',
 ['Jesus contrasts worship confined to Mount Gerizim or Jerusalem with worship "in spirit and in truth" that is open everywhere (4:21). He is not abolishing visible worship.',
  'The heavenly worship shown in Revelation has an altar, incense, vestments, harps, hymns and prostration (Revelation 4–5; 8:3–4).',
  'The Incarnation means God acts through visible things: water, bread, wine, oil, the laying on of hands.',
  'Every Christian tradition uses some outward forms: buildings, music, set times, bread and wine. The question is whether the outward expresses the inward.'],
 'Worship in spirit and truth is worship in the Holy Spirit and in Christ, who is the Truth, and it takes body in the liturgy.',
 'CCC 1145–1162, 1179; Revelation 4–5',
 tags=('mass','images'))
D('john',4,24,
 ctx="John 4: Jesus speaks with the Samaritan woman at Jacob’s well. She raises the dispute between Samaritans and Jews: ‘Our fathers adored on this mountain [Gerizim], and you say, that at Jerusalem is the place where men must adore.’ Jesus answers: ‘Woman, believe me, that the hour cometh, when you shall neither on this mountain, nor in Jerusalem, adore the Father… But the hour cometh, and now is, when the true adorers shall adore the Father in spirit and in truth. For the Father also seeketh such to adore him. God is a spirit; and they that adore him, must adore him in spirit and in truth’ (4:20–24). She speaks of the Messiah; Jesus replies: ‘I am he’ (4:26).",
 who="Used by Puritan and Reformed traditions (the ‘regulative principle’ of worship: only what Scripture commands is allowed) and by many evangelicals against liturgical, sacramental and sensory worship. Spiritual worship means sincere inner devotion, not ritual.",
 lang="“In spirit and in truth” (en pneumati kai alētheia): in John, ‘Spirit’ is the Holy Spirit given by Christ (7:39; 14:17, ‘the Spirit of truth’), and ‘truth’ is Christ himself (14:6, ‘I am the way, and the truth’; 1:14, ‘full of grace and truth’). Jesus is announcing a new kind of worship made possible by himself and the Spirit, not a contrast between inner and outer.",
 steps=[
  ["The contrast is between places, not between inner and outer","The question was where to worship: Gerizim or Jerusalem. Jesus answers that the time is coming when worship will not be tied to one holy mountain, but offered by the true worshippers everywhere: the fulfilment of Malachi 1:11 (‘in every place… a clean oblation’)."],
  ["Spirit and truth are the Holy Spirit and Christ","True worship is in the Holy Spirit and in Christ, who is the Truth and the new Temple (2:19–21: ‘he spoke of the temple of his body’). Christian liturgy is exactly this: worship of the Father, through Christ, in the Spirit (CCC 1110)."],
  ["Heavenly worship is full of signs","Revelation shows the elders prostrating, casting crowns, holding harps and ‘golden vials full of odours, which are the prayers of saints’ (5:8), an angel at a golden altar with a censer (8:3–4), white robes (7:9), hymns (4:8; 5:9–14). If spiritual worship excluded signs, heaven would not be spiritual."],
  ["Christ worships with visible signs","He commanded baptism with water, the Eucharist with bread and wine, the laying on of hands and anointing (Mark 6:13; James 5:14). He used spittle and mud to heal (John 9:6) and attended the Temple feasts. God became flesh; the material world is the means he chose."],
  ["The inner must animate the outer","Catholics agree that external ritual without faith and love is worthless (Isaiah 29:13; Matthew 15:8). The liturgy is meant to express and nourish the heart’s worship (Sacrosanctum Concilium 11: the faithful must take part ‘with right dispositions… their minds attuned to their voices’)."]],
 fathers=[
  ["Thomas Aquinas, ST II-II q.81 a.7; q.84 a.2","External worship is ordered to internal worship: we use bodily signs because we are bodily creatures, and they stir up devotion (paraphrased)."],
  ["Thomas Aquinas, Commentary on John, ch. 4, lect. 2","‘In spirit’: with the fervour of the Holy Spirit; ‘in truth’: not in figures, as the Old Law did, but in the reality of Christ (paraphrased)."],
  ["Sacrosanctum Concilium 7–8 (1963)","In the earthly liturgy we share by foretaste in the heavenly liturgy of the holy city."]],
 obj=[
  ["“Only what Scripture commands is allowed in worship.”","Scripture does not command church buildings, hymnals, pulpits, Sunday schools or worship bands either. And Scripture shows heavenly worship with incense and altar, which the earthly Church imitates."]],
 talk="“The Samaritan woman was asking where to worship: her mountain or Jerusalem. Jesus says soon it won’t be one holy mountain; it’ll be in the Spirit and in the Truth, which is himself. That’s what the Mass is: worship of the Father, through Christ, in the Spirit, all over the world. And the worship of heaven in Revelation has an altar, incense, robes and singing. Bodies and signs aren’t the opposite of spirit; they’re how embodied people worship.”",
 avoid="Do not defend liturgy as merely aesthetic; ground it in the Incarnation and in Revelation.",
 read=["CCC 1136–1162","Sacrosanctum Concilium 5–13","Joseph Ratzinger (2000) The Spirit of the Liturgy","Scott Hahn (1999) The Lamb’s Supper"])
